            <ArticleTitle>Therapeutic Hotline: Cysteinyl leukotriene receptor antagonist montelukast in the treatment of atopic dermatitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>90-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1529-8019.2009.01295.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Leukotrienes are potent proinflammatory mediators derived from arachidonic acid through the 5-lipoxygenase pathway. Experimental data suggest a role for cysteinyl leukotrienes in the pathogenesis of atopy giving a rationale for its use in asthma, allergic rhinitis, and chronic urticaria management. A few clinical observations and small trials suggest that montelukast may be used in an adjunctive manner as an effective therapeutic option for all age categories affected by moderate-to-severe atopic dermatitis. Our own observations proved that montelukast as a prospective corticosteroid-sparing option in the complex therapeutic strategy of corticosteroid-dependent atopic dermatitis patients, even in the severe erythrodermic cases.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
